  "services": {
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "The Marquee: 30 Legendary Years is a compilation album released by Polydor in 1989 that celebrates three decades of performances at The Marquee, a legendary London venue central to British rock history. The collection spans multiple generations of rock and pop artists who performed at the club, featuring studio recordings rather than live tracks from acts including Queen, U2, Genesis, The Jam, The Who, Dire Straits, Pink Floyd, The Police, and Bryan Adams. With 32 tracks across a double vinyl format, the album presents a broad survey of rock and pop styles that defined the venue's cultural significance.\n\nThe compilation takes a retrospective rather than chronological approach to its curation, assembling songs that represent artists associated with The Marquee across its history rather than capturing performances from specific eras when those artists actually played there. This approach results in an eclectic mix that prioritizes recognizable studio versions and chart-friendly tracks over historical accuracy or thematic coherence. The album includes edited versions of certain tracks, such as U2's \"Pride (In the Name of Love),\" which would become a recurring feature on subsequent Universal compilations.\n\nThe album serves as a nostalgic document of The Marquee's influence on British popular music, functioning as both a tribute to the venue and a sampler of major rock and pop acts from the 1970s and 1980s. While primarily a commercial compilation aimed at collectors and casual listeners rather than a critically acclaimed release, it captures the breadth of talent that passed through one of rock music's most important performance spaces.",
      "generated_at": "2025-11-27T17:01:26.210880",
      "model": "sonar",
      "source": "perplexity"
    }
  },
